WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.744
TTL and soft delete showed how
data goes away when you ask it to.

2
00:00:04.744 --> 00:00:07.944
Here, we stop it going away
when a silo restarts.

3
00:00:08.294 --> 00:00:13.241
This is Going durable: durable grain
storage and a durable write-ahead log,

4
00:00:13.241 --> 00:00:15.550
in place of the in-memory defaults.

5
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:19.493
Every episode so far has run
on the defaults,

6
00:00:19.493 --> 00:00:22.306
and the defaults keep everything
in memory.

7
00:00:22.656 --> 00:00:24.572
A tree lives in two places.

8
00:00:24.572 --> 00:00:28.190
Grain storage holds its leaves
and their snapshots,

9
00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:32.588
and the write-ahead log holds
every committed write, in order.

10
00:00:32.588 --> 00:00:38.051
In memory, neither survives a restart,
so going durable means replacing both.

11
00:00:39.051 --> 00:00:40.493
Start with grain storage.

12
00:00:40.493 --> 00:00:44.991
The callback you pass when you register
Lattice chooses it, so swap the memory

13
00:00:44.991 --> 00:00:48.797
provider for a durable Orleans provider,
here Azure Table storage.

14
00:00:49.147 --> 00:00:53.939
That provider must enforce ETags, so
that a write presenting a stale one fails.

15
00:00:53.939 --> 00:00:58.125
Orleans' own durable providers do,
and each silo checks as it starts:

16
00:00:58.125 --> 00:01:02.917
by default, a provider that accepts a
stale write stops the silo from starting.

17
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:05.481
Then the write-ahead log.

18
00:01:05.481 --> 00:01:07.046
With no cloud dependency,

19
00:01:07.046 --> 00:01:11.614
the file provider keeps it on local disk:
one call, and a root directory.

20
00:01:11.964 --> 00:01:15.214
It suits a single container
with a mounted volume.

21
00:01:15.214 --> 00:01:20.609
Each batch is all or nothing, so a crash
before a batch is committed rolls it back.

22
00:01:21.609 --> 00:01:24.765
For production deployments replicated
across regions,

23
00:01:24.765 --> 00:01:28.694
the Azure Table provider keeps the log
in a storage table instead.

24
00:01:28.694 --> 00:01:31.016
Either call replaces the in-memory log,

25
00:01:31.016 --> 00:01:34.291
whether it comes before
or after Lattice is registered.

26
00:01:34.641 --> 00:01:37.653
Either way,
the log is only half of a durable tree.

27
00:01:37.653 --> 00:01:41.020
It is trimmed once a snapshot
in grain storage covers it,

28
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.978
so pair it with durable grain storage too,
not the memory provider.

29
00:01:46.778 --> 00:01:49.855
Next on Build:
Moving in from another store.

30
00:01:49.855 --> 00:01:53.910
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.
